brsoal\" style=\"\"><br></p>','','','','','','','-'),(34239,'853',39,'PAT_9512','uraian','<p><img src=\"../gambar/34/6a07bfe3ea440.jpg\" id=\"gbrsoal\" style=\"\"><br></p>','','','','','','','-'),(34240,'853',40,'PAT_9512','uraian','<p><img src=\"../gambar/34/6a07c030bd480.jpg\" id=\"gbrsoal\" style=\"\"><br></p>','','','','','','','-'),(34241,'821',22,'PAT_9515','pg','<p>Beyond the Ground: Elevating Street Art</p><p>\r\n<div>\r\n</div><div>Street art has transcended conventional boundaries through graffiti-defying creations. By utilizing walls and vertical surfaces as canvases, artists challenge graffiti and engage viewers. This technique empowers artists to push creative limits and craft immersive experiences.\r\n</div><div>Graffiti art enriches the interaction between artists, artwork and surroundings. By incorporating urban architecture, artists initiate dialogues between art and public spaces. This heightens awareness of art\'s significance in daily life and invites viewers to participate in the creative process.\r\n</div><div>Notable examples include Banksy\'s and Shepard Fairey\'s works, leveraging graffiti art to convey powerful social and political messages. Thus, graffiti art becomes not only artistic expression but also a catalyst for social change.\r\n</div><div><br></div><div>The word “empowers” is opposite in meaning (antonym) to…</div></p>','supports','strengthens','weakens','encourages','inspires\r\n<div><br></div>','','C'),(34242,'821',23,'PAT_9515','pg','<p>Beyond the Ground: Elevating Street Art</p><p>\r\n<div>\r\n</div><div>Street art has transcended conventional boundaries through graffiti-defying creations. By utilizing walls and vertical surfaces as canvases, artists challenge graffiti and engage viewers. This technique empowers artists to push creative limits and craft immersive experiences.\r\n</div><div>Graffiti art enriches the interaction between artists, artwork and surroundings. By incorporating urban architecture, artists initiate dialogues between art and public spaces. This heightens awareness of art\'s significance in daily life and invites viewers to participate in the creative process.\r\n</div><div>Notable examples include Banksy\'s and Shepard Fairey\'s works, leveraging graffiti art to convey powerful social and political messages. Thus, graffiti art becomes not only artistic expression but also a catalyst for social change.\r\n</div><div><br></div><div>The word “immersive” is closest in meaning (synonym) to…</div></p>','engaging','boring','simple','ordinary\r\n<div><br></div>','distant','','A'),(34243,'821',24,'PAT_9515','pg','<p>Beyond the Ground: Elevating Street Art</p><p>\r\n<div>\r\n</div><div>Street art has transcended conventional boundaries through graffiti-defying creations. By utilizing walls and vertical surfaces as canvases, artists challenge graffiti and engage viewers. This technique empowers artists to push creative limits and craft immersive experiences.\r\n</div><div>Graffiti art enriches the interaction between artists, artwork and surroundings. By incorporating urban architecture, artists initiate dialogues between art and public spaces. This heightens awareness of art\'s significance in daily life and invites viewers to participate in the creative process.\r\n</div><div>Notable examples include Banksy\'s and Shepard Fairey\'s works, leveraging graffiti art to convey powerful social and political messages. Thus, graffiti art becomes not only artistic expression but also a catalyst for social change.\r\n</div><div><br></div><div>The word “enriches” is opposite in meaning (antonym) to…</div></p>','improves','enhances','develops','damages<div><br></div>','increases','','D'),(34244,'821',25,'PAT_9515','pg','<p>Beyond the Ground: Elevating Street Art</p><p>\r\n<div>\r\n</div><div>Street art has transcended conventional boundaries through graffiti-defying creations. By utilizing walls and vertical surfaces as canvases, artists challenge graffiti and engage viewers. This technique empowers artists to push creative limits and craft immersive experiences.\r\n</div><div>Graffiti art enriches the interaction between artists, artwork and surroundings. By incorporating urban architecture, artists initiate dialogues between art and public spaces. This heightens awareness of art\'s significance in daily life and invites viewers to participate in the creative process.\r\n</div><div>Notable examples include Banksy\'s and Shepard Fairey\'s works, leveraging graffiti art to convey powerful social and political messages. Thus, graffiti art becomes not only artistic expression but also a catalyst for social change.\r\n</div><div><br></div><div>The word “significance” is closest in meaning (synonym) to…</div><div>\r\n</div></p>','importance','difficulty','weakness','confusion','failure\r\n<div><br></div>','','A'),(34245,'821',26,'PAT_9515','pg','<p>History of Graffiti: Is it Art?</p><p>\r\n<div>The term graffiti comes from the Greek, word of \'graphein\' which means \'to write\'. People have always drawn and painted on walls. Prehistoric man painted on the walls of caves, and those paintings are now considered valuable works of art. Graffiti was first found on ancient Roman architecture.\r\n</div><div>If people draw on walls these days, it isn\'t considered art; it is called \'graffiti\' and often cleaned off as soon as possible. Graffiti art takes a number of forms. It can be an individual mark, usually just the initials of the artist, or something much larger, like a mural. Most graffiti artists prefer to be called \'writers\'. A writer\'s signature with spray paint or maker is called a \'tag\', and each piece of graffiti art is \'tagged\'. Communities of writers who are friends are known as \'crews\', and inexperienced writers are called \'toys\'.\r\n</div><div>For some artists graffiti is a reaction to the circumstances of their lives, while for others it is a more thought-out artistic expression. The motivation behind a piece of graffiti can be happiness or sadness, frustration or relief. For this reason, it is an art form that is very close to the hearts of the young.\r\n</div><div>Graffiti as a youth culture began in America but has become popular in many other countries. Its influences, come from pop culture especially music and cartoons. Graffiti is now so popular; it can be seen in many museums and art galleries.\r\n</div><div><br></div><div>What is the social function (purpose) of an analytical exposition text?</div></p>','To entertain the reader with a story','To describe a particular person or place','To persuade the reader that something is the case','To explain how something works<div><br></div>','To retell past experiences','','C'),(34246,'821',27,'PAT_9515','pg','<p>History of Graffiti: Is it Art?</p><p>\r\n<div>The term graffiti comes from the Greek, word of \'graphein\' which means \'to write\'. People have always drawn and painted on walls. Prehistoric man painted on the walls of caves, and those paintings are now considered valuable works of art. Graffiti was first found on ancient Roman architecture.\r\n</div><div>If people draw on walls these days, it isn\'t considered art; it is called \'graffiti\' and often cleaned off as soon as possible. Graffiti art takes a number of forms. It can be an individual mark, usually just the initials of the artist, or something much larger, like a mural. Most graffiti artists prefer to be called \'writers\'. A writer\'s signature with spray paint or maker is called a \'tag\', and each piece of graffiti art is \'tagged\'. Communities of writers who are friends are known as \'crews\', and inexperienced writers are called \'toys\'.\r\n</div><div>For some artists graffiti is a reaction to the circumstances of their lives, while for others it is a more thought-out artistic expression. The motivation behind a piece of graffiti can be happiness or sadness, frustration or relief. For this reason, it is an art form that is very close to the hearts of the young.\r\n</div><div>Graffiti as a youth culture began in America but has become popular in many other countries. Its influences, come from pop culture especially music and cartoons. Graffiti is now so popular; it can be seen in many museums and art galleries.\r\n</div><div><br></div><div>Which part of analytical exposition contains the writer’s arguments?</div></p>','Orientation','Complication','Arguments','Resolution<div><br></div>','Recount','','C'),(34247,'821',28,'PAT_9515','pg','<p>History of Graffiti: Is it Art?</p><p>\r\n<div>The term graffiti comes from the Greek, word of \'graphein\' which means \'to write\'. People have always drawn and painted on walls. Prehistoric man painted on the walls of caves, and those paintings are now considered valuable works of art. Graffiti was first found on ancient Roman architecture.\r\n</div><div>If people draw on walls these days, it isn\'t considered art; it is called \'graffiti\' and often cleaned off as soon as possible. Graffiti art takes a number of forms. It can be an individual mark, usually just the initials of the artist, or something much larger, like a mural. Most graffiti artists prefer to be called \'writers\'. A writer\'s signature with spray paint or maker is called a \'tag\', and each piece of graffiti art is \'tagged\'. Communities of writers who are friends are known as \'crews\', and inexperienced writers are called \'toys\'.\r\n</div><div>For some artists graffiti is a reaction to the circumstances of their lives, while for others it is a more thought-out artistic expression. The motivation behind a piece of graffiti can be happiness or sadness, frustration or relief. For this reason, it is an art form that is very close to the hearts of the young.\r\n</div><div>Graffiti as a youth culture began in America but has become popular in many other countries. Its influences, come from pop culture especially music and cartoons. Graffiti is now so popular; it can be seen in many museums and art galleries.</div><div><br></div><div>\r\n</div><div>What is an analytical exposition text?</div></p>','A text that tells past events in chronological order<div><br></div>','A text that describes a person, place, or thing in detail','A text that explains how to do something step by step','A text that presents arguments to persuade the reader that something is true','A text that entertains readers with imaginative stories','','D'),(34248,'821',29,'PAT_9515','pg','<p>History of Graffiti: Is it Art?</p><p>\r\n<div>The term graffiti comes from the Greek, word of \'graphein\' which means \'to write\'. People have always drawn and painted on walls. Prehistoric man painted on the walls of caves, and those paintings are now considered valuable works of art. Graffiti was first found on ancient Roman architecture.\r\n</div><div>If people draw on walls these days, it isn\'t considered art; it is called \'graffiti\' and often cleaned off as soon as possible. Graffiti art takes a number of forms. It can be an individual mark, usually just the initials of the artist, or something much larger, like a mural. Most graffiti artists prefer to be called \'writers\'. A writer\'s signature with spray paint or maker is called a \'tag\', and each piece of graffiti art is \'tagged\'. Communities of writers who are friends are known as \'crews\', and inexperienced writers are called \'toys\'.\r\n</div><div>For some artists graffiti is a reaction to the circumstances of their lives, while for others it is a more thought-out artistic expression. The motivation behind a piece of graffiti can be happiness or sadness, frustration or relief. For this reason, it is an art form that is very close to the hearts of the young.\r\n</div><div>Graffiti as a youth culture began in America but has become popular in many other countries. Its influences, come from pop culture especially music and cartoons. Graffiti is now so popular; it can be seen in many museums and art galleries.\r\n</div><div><br></div><div>What does the text suggest about valuable works of art?</div></p>','Valuable works of art only come from modern cities.','Valuable works of art include prehistoric paintings on cave walls.','Valuable works of art are always created by professional artists.<div><br></div>','Valuable works of art are usually graffiti found on buildings today.','Valuable works of art only exist in museums and galleries.','','B'),(34249,'821',30,'PAT_9515','pg','<p>History of Graffiti: Is it Art?</p><p>\r\n<div>The term graffiti comes from the Greek, word of \'graphein\' which means \'to write\'. People have always drawn and painted on walls. Prehistoric man painted on the walls of caves, and those paintings are now considered valuable works of art. Graffiti was first found on ancient Roman architecture.\r\n</div><div>If people draw on walls these days, it isn\'t considered art; it is called \'graffiti\' and often cleaned off as soon as possible. Graffiti art takes a number of forms. It can be an individual mark, usually just the initials of the artist, or something much larger, like a mural. Most graffiti artists prefer to be called \'writers\'. A writer\'s signature with spray paint or maker is called a \'tag\', and each piece of graffiti art is \'tagged\'. Communities of writers who are friends are known as \'crews\', and inexperienced writers are called \'toys\'.\r\n</div><div>For some artists graffiti is a reaction to the circumstances of their lives, while for others it is a more thought-out artistic expression. The motivation behind a piece of graffiti can be happiness or sadness, frustration or relief. For this reason, it is an art form that is very close to the hearts of the young.\r\n</div><div>Graffiti as a youth culture began in America but has become popular in many other countries. Its influences, come from pop culture especially music and cartoons. Graffiti is now so popular; it can be seen in many museums and art galleries.\r\n</div><div><br></div><div>What is the main idea of the second paragraph?</div></p>','The history of graffiti in ancient times','The influence of music on graffiti culture','The reasons why young people like graffiti','The popularity of graffiti in museums<div><br></div>','Different forms and terms used in graffiti art','','E'),(34250,'821',31,'PAT_9515','pg','He does not like graffiti.\r\n<div>The correct interrogative form is…</div><div><br></div>','Does she like graffiti?','Do she like graffiti?','Is she like graffiti?','Does she likes graffiti?','Did she like graffiti?','','A'),(34251,'821',32,'PAT_9515','pg','Is she happy with the graffiti result?\r\n<div>The correct positive sentence is…</div>','She am happy with the graffiti result.','She is happy with the graffiti result.','She are happy with the graffiti result.','She happy with the graffiti result.<div><br></div>','She be happy with the graffiti result.','','B'),(34252,'821',33,'PAT_9515','pg','She is happy with the painting.\r\n<div>The correct negative and interrogative forms are…</div>','She is not happy with the painting. / Is she happy with the painting?','She not is happy with the painting. / Does she happy with the painting?','She are not happy with the painting. / Are she happy with the painting?','<p>&nbsp;She is not happy with the painting. / Do she happy with the painting?</p>','She am not happy with the painting. / Am she happy with the painting?\r\n<div><br></div>','','A'),(34253,'821',34,'PAT_9515','pg','<p>Read the statements below carefully!</p><p><div>1. Narrative text tells readers about past events.</div><div>2. Narrative text aims to entertain and teach moral values.</div><div>3. Narrative text only explains scientific facts.</div><div>4. Narrative text usually contains conflicts and resolutions.</div><div><br></div><div>Which statements BEST describe a narrative text?</div></p>','1 and 2','2 and 3','2 and 4','1, 2, and 4','1, 3, and 4','','D'),(34254,'821',35,'PAT_9515','pg','What is the main purpose of a narrative text?','To explain how to make something','To entertain readers through a story and sometimes give moral lessons','To describe a particular person or place in detail','To inform readers about scientific facts','To persuade readers to agree with an opinion','','B'),(34255,'821',36,'PAT_9515','pg','The Greedy Farmer<div><br></div><div>A farmer found a goose that laid a golden egg every morning. At first, he was very happy because he became rich slowly. However, after several months, he became impatient. He thought that there must be many golden eggs inside the goose’s body.</div><div>One day, he killed the goose and cut it open. Unfortunately, he found nothing inside. Realizing his mistake, the farmer cried because he had lost the goose forever.</div><div><br></div><div>Why did the farmer kill the goose?</div>','He wanted to sell the goose immediately','He was afraid the goose would run away','He hoped to get all the golden eggs at once','He wanted to give the goose to someone else','He thought the goose was dangerous','','C'),(34256,'821',37,'PAT_9515','pg','The Greedy Farmer<div><br></div><div>A farmer found a goose that laid a golden egg every morning. At first, he was very happy because he became rich slowly. However, after several months, he became impatient. He thought that there must be many golden eggs inside the goose’s body.</div><div>One day, he killed the goose and cut it open. Unfortunately, he found nothing inside. Realizing his mistake, the farmer cried because he had lost the goose forever.</div><div><br></div><div>Which statement BEST explains the moral value of the story?</div>','Wealth always brings happiness','Greed can lead to loss','Animals should be protected','Farmers must work harder','Gold is more important than honesty','','B'),(34257,'821',38,'PAT_9515','pg','The Boy Who Cried Wolf<div><br></div><div>A shepherd boy was bored while watching his sheep near a village. To amuse himself, he shouted, “Wolf! Wolf!” The villagers ran to help him, but they found no wolf at all. The boy laughed at them.</div><div>A few days later, he repeated the same trick. Again, the villagers came and realized that the boy had lied. They became angry and warned him not to do it again.</div><div>One afternoon, a real wolf appeared and attacked the sheep. The boy screamed for help as loudly as he could, but this time no one believed him. In the end, many sheep were lost.</div><div><br></div><div>Why did the villagers ignore the boy’s last cry for help?</div>','They were too busy working','They did not hear the boy’s voice','They believed the wolf was harmless','They no longer trusted the boy','They wanted to punish the sheep','','D'),(34258,'821',39,'PAT_9515','pg','The Village of Endless Light<div><br></div><div>Long ago, there was a small village surrounded by mountains. The villagers depended on oil lamps to light their houses at night. Unfortunately, oil was expensive and difficult to find.</div><div>A clever girl named Amira wanted to help her village. One day, she noticed the strong flow of water in the river and got an idea to create a water wheel that could produce electricity.</div><div>Many villagers doubted her idea, but Amira continued working hard. After several weeks, the water wheel finally produced electricity for some houses. The villagers were amazed and began working together to build more water wheels.</div><div>Since then, the village had enough energy, and children could study comfortably at night.</div><div><br></div><div>Why did Amira decide to create a water wheel?</div>','She wanted to become famous in the village','She was interested in building toys','She wanted to solve the village’s energy problem','She wanted to sell electricity to other villages','She was forced by the villagers','','C'),(34259,'821',40,'PAT_9515','pg','The Village of Endless Light<div><br></div><div>Long ago, there was a small village surrounded by mountains. The villagers depended on oil lamps to light their houses at night. Unfortunately, oil was expensive and difficult to find.</div><div>A clever girl named Amira wanted to help her village. One day, she noticed the strong flow of water in the river and got an idea to create a water wheel that could produce electricity.</div><div>Many villagers doubted her idea, but Amira continued working hard. After several weeks, the water wheel finally produced electricity for some houses. The villagers were amazed and began working together to build more water wheels.</div><div>Since then, the village had enough energy, and children could study comfortably at night.</div><div><br></div><div>Why did the villagers doubt Amira at first?</div>','They did not believe a young girl could solve the problem','They hated new technology','They already had enough electricity','They wanted to leave the village','They were afraid of the river','','A'),(34260,'821',41,'PAT_9515','uraian','Graffiti\'s Gray Area: Vandalism or Artistic Expression\r\n<div><br></div><div>Graffiti is often viewed as vandalism due to property damage. However, can all graffiti be classified as criminal acts? Or does it hold artistic and social value? These sparks debate about graffiti\'s status as art.\r\n</div><div>Graffiti can be authentic, spontaneous artistic expression. Artists utilize their work to convey vital social and political messages. Yet, without property owner consent, graffiti is considered vandalism.\r\n</div><div>Some graffiti has gained recognition as legitimate art, protected by law. Jean-Michel Basquiat\'s and Keith Haring\'s works demonstrate significant contributions. Therefore, graffiti\'s status warrants reevaluation.</div><div><br></div><div>\r\n</div><div>What is your opinion about the arguments presented in the text regarding graffiti as both vandalism and artistic expression? Do you think the arguments are convincing? Explain your reasons with supporting ideas.</div>','','','','','','','In my opinion, the arguments in the text are convincing because graffiti can have both negative and positive aspects. It is true that graffiti without permission can damage property and be considered vandalism. However, graffiti can also be a powerful form of artistic expression that communicates social and political messages. Many famous artists started with graffiti, which shows its artistic value. Therefore, I believe graffiti should not always be judged as a crime.'),(34261,'821',42,'PAT_9515','uraian','<p>Graffiti\'s Gray Area: Vandalism or Artistic Expression</p><p>\r\n<div>\r\n</div><div>Graffiti is often viewed as vandalism due to property damage. However, can all graffiti be classified as criminal acts? Or does it hold artistic and social value? These sparks debate about graffiti\'s status as art.\r\n</div><div>Graffiti can be authentic, spontaneous artistic expression. Artists utilize their work to convey vital social and political messages. Yet, without property owner consent, graffiti is considered vandalism.\r\n</div><div>Some graffiti has gained recognition as legitimate art, protected by law. Jean-Michel Basquiat\'s and Keith Haring\'s works demonstrate significant contributions. Therefore, graffiti\'s status warrants reevaluation.\r\n</div><div><br></div><div>The text mentions Jean-Michel Basquiat and Keith Haring. Why are they mentioned?\r\n</div><div><br></div></p>','','','','','','','They are mentioned because they are famous artists. Their success shows that graffiti can be real art.'),(34262,'841',1,'PAT_9360','pg','Perhatikan data di bawah ini! \r\n<div>1). Tumbuhan \r\n</div><div>2). Matahari \r\n</div><div>3). Tanah \r\n</div><div>4). Suhu \r\n</div><div>5). Hewan \r\n</div><div>6). Bakteri \r\n</div><div>Berdasarkan data tersebut, komponen abiotik dalam ekosistem ditunjukkan oleh nomor...&nbsp;</div>','1), 2), dan 3)','2), 3), dan 4)&nbsp;','2), 3), dan 6)&nbsp;','3), 4), dan 5)','1), 5), dan 6)','','B'),(34263,'841',2,'PAT_9360','pg','Di sebuah hutan hujan tropis, peneliti mengamati rantai makanan yang melibatkan pohon buah, serangga, katak, dan ular. Suatu ketika, terjadi lonjakan musiman pada populasi serangga secara drastis di hutan tersebut. Berdasarkan konsep aliran energi dan interaksi makhluk hidup, dampak langsung yang akan terjadi pada komponen biotik lainnya di dalam ekosistem tersebut adalah...','Populasi pohon buah akan meningkat pesat karena penyerbukan terbantu','Populasi katak akan meningkat karena ketersediaan sumber makanan melimpah','Populasi ular akan langsung menurun drastis karena kekurangan makanan','Energi yang mengalir ke tingkat trofik di atas serangga akan berkurang','Bakteri pengurai akan berhenti menguraikan materi organik di tanah','','B'),(34264,'841',3,'PAT_9360','pg','Di sebuah perkebunan, seorang petani menyemprotkan fungisida dan bakterisida kimia dosis tinggi secara terus-menerus. Tindakan ini menyebabkan sebagian besar populasi jamur dan bakteri yang berperan sebagai pengurai di dalam tanah mati. Berdasarkan konsep daur materi di ekosistem, dampak nyata yang akan segera terjadi pada perkebunan tersebut adalah...','Produktivitas tanaman meningkat karena tanah terbebas dari mikroorganisme','Terhentinya proses penguraian sisa daun mati menjadi unsur hara tanah','Akar tanaman akan lebih cepat menyerap air dan oksigen dari dalam tanah','Tanaman tidak dapat lagi melakukan proses fotosintesis secara optimal','Pasokan gas nitrogen di atmosfer sekitar perkebunan akan melonjak drastis','','B'),(34265,'821',43,'PAT_9515','uraian','The Power of the Sun<div><br></div><div>One day, a small village often had blackouts at night. The villagers could not study or work properly because there was not enough electricity.</div><div>A smart student named Bima thought about using sunlight as energy. He collected some old solar panels from a nearby city and tried to install them on the roof of his school. At first, many people doubted him because they thought the project was too difficult.</div><div>However, Bima kept learning and working hard. After several weeks, the solar panels finally worked. The school could produce electricity from sunlight, and students were able to study comfortably again.</div><div>The villagers were proud of Bima and started using solar energy in their homes. They realized that the sun could become a useful and environmentally friendly source of energy.</div><div><br></div><div>Why did many villagers doubt Bima’s idea at first?</div>','','','','','','','Because they thought producing electricity from sunlight was too difficult and impossible for a student.'),(34266,'821',44,'PAT_9515','uraian','The Power of the Sun<div><br></div><div>One day, a small village often had blackouts at night. The villagers could not study or work properly because there was not enough electricity.</div><div>A smart student named Bima thought about using sunlight as energy. He collected some old solar panels from a nearby city and tried to install them on the roof of his school. At first, many people doubted him because they thought the project was too difficult.</div><div>However, Bima kept learning and working hard. After several weeks, the solar panels finally worked. The school could produce electricity from sunlight, and students were able to study comfortably again.</div><div>The villagers were proud of Bima and started using solar energy in their homes. They realized that the sun could become a useful and environmentally friendly source of energy.</div><div><br></div><div>What can readers learn from Bima’s character?</div>','','','','','','','Readers can learn to be creative, hardworking, and never give up when solving problems.'),(34267,'841',4,'PAT_9360','pg','Taman Nasional Ujung Kulon merupakan kawasan konservasi yang memiliki berbagai jenis flora dan fauna. Dalam suatu kegiatan penelitian, seorang peneliti melakukan pengamatan untuk mendata keanekaragaman hayati di wilayah tersebut. Selama pengamatan, peneliti mencatat keberadaan beberapa satwa seperti badak jawa, rusa, burung rangkong, dan kera ekor panjang. Pada salah satu titik pengamatan, peneliti melihat 15 ekor kera ekor panjang berada di area yang sama dan beraktivitas bersama dalam waktu yang sama. Berdasarkan pengamatan tersebut, sekelompok kera ekor panjang yang ditemukan peneliti disebut sebagai…','Individu','Populasi','Komunitas','Ekosistem','Bioma','','B'),(34268,'821',45,'PAT_9515','uraian','The Power of the Sun<div><br></div><div>One day, a small village often had blackouts at night. The villagers could not study or work properly because there was not enough electricity.</div><div>A smart student named Bima thought about using sunlight as energy. He collected some old solar panels from a nearby city and tried to install them on the roof of his school. At first, many people doubted him because they thought the project was too difficult.</div><div>However, Bima kept learning and working hard. After several weeks, the solar panels finally worked. The school could produce electricity from sunlight, and students were able to study comfortably again.</div><div>The villagers were proud of Bima and started using solar energy in their homes. They realized that the sun could become a useful and environmentally friendly source of energy.</div><div><br></div><div>In your opinion, how can students help save energy at school?</div>','','','','','','','Students can turn off lights and electronic devices when not used and support the use of renewable energy like solar panels.'),(34269,'822',1,'PAT_9516','pg','<p>Graffiti: Art or Problem?</p><p>\r\n<div>Graffiti is common in many cities today. Some people think graffiti is art, but others think it is a problem. I believe graffiti on public places without permission should be banned.\r\n</div><div>First, graffiti makes public places look dirty and damaged. Cleaning it also costs money. Second, too much graffiti can make people feel uncomfortable because the area looks unsafe. However, graffiti can be positive if it is made legally in special areas for artists.\r\n</div><div>In conclusion, graffiti without permission should not be allowed, but legal graffiti can still be appreciated as art.\r\n</div><div><br></div><div>What is the writer’s main opinion?</div></p>','Graffiti is always beautiful art.','Graffiti should be free everywhere.','Graffiti without permission should be banned.','Graffiti artists should be arrested.<div><br></div>','Graffiti has no negative impact.','','C'),(34270,'822',2,'PAT_9516','pg','<div>Graffiti: Art or Problem?</div><div><br></div><div>\r\n</div><div>Graffiti is common in many cities today. Some people think graffiti is art, but others think it is a problem. I believe graffiti on public places without permission should be banned.\r\n</div><div>First, graffiti makes public places look dirty and damaged. Cleaning it also costs money. Second, too much graffiti can make people feel uncomfortable because the area looks unsafe. However, graffiti can be positive if it is made legally in special areas for artists.\r\n</div><div>In conclusion, graffiti without permission should not be allowed, but legal graffiti can still be appreciated as art.\r\n</div><div><br></div><div>Why does the writer mention cleaning costs?</div>','To show graffiti artists are rich','To explain that graffiti causes financial problems','To compare graffiti with other art<div><br></div>','To describe city budgets','To show graffiti is popular','','B'),(34271,'822',3,'PAT_9516','pg','<p>Graffiti: Art or Problem?</p><p>\r\n<div>Graffiti is common in many cities today. Some people think graffiti is art, but others think it is a problem. I believe graffiti on public places without permission should be banned.\r\n</div><div>First, graffiti makes public places look dirty and damaged. Cleaning it also costs money. Second, too much graffiti can make people feel uncomfortable because the area looks unsafe. However, graffiti can be positive if it is made legally in special areas for artists.\r\n</div><div>In conclusion, graffiti without permission should not be allowed, but legal graf