','A'),(34278,'822',10,'PAT_9516','pg','<p>Graffiti: Art or Problem?</p><p>\r\n<div>Graffiti is common in many cities today. Some people think graffiti is art, but others think it is a problem. I believe graffiti on public places without permission should be banned.\r\n</div><div>First, graffiti makes public places look dirty and damaged. Cleaning it also costs money. Second, too much graffiti can make people feel uncomfortable because the area looks unsafe. However, graffiti can be positive if it is made legally in special areas for artists.\r\n</div><div>In conclusion, graffiti without permission should not be allowed, but legal graffiti can still be appreciated as art.\r\n</div><div><br></div><div>The best alternative title for the text is…</div></p>','Famous Graffiti Artists','City Cleaning Costs','Controlling Graffiti in Public Places','Teenagers and Hobbies<div><br></div>','Modern Art History','','C'),(34279,'822',11,'PAT_9516','pg','<p>The Honest Woodcutter</p><p>\r\n<div>Once upon a time, a poor woodcutter lived near a forest. One day, while cutting wood beside a river, his axe slipped from his hands and fell into the deep water. The woodcutter sat sadly because he could not afford to buy a new axe.\r\n</div><div>Suddenly, a fairy appeared and offered to help him. The fairy dived into the river and brought out a golden axe. The woodcutter shook his head and said it was not his. Then the fairy brought out a silver axe, but again he refused it. Finally, the fairy brought out an old iron axe, and the woodcutter happily claimed it as his own.\r\n</div><div>The fairy was impressed by his honesty and gave him all three axes as a reward.\r\n</div><div><br></div><div>What can be inferred about the woodcutter’s character?</div></p>','He was greedy and selfish','He valued honesty more than wealth','He disliked expensive things','He was afraid of the fairy<div><br></div>','He wanted to impress others','','B'),(34280,'822',12,'PAT_9516','pg','<p>The Honest Woodcutter</p><p>\r\n<div>Once upon a time, a poor woodcutter lived near a forest. One day, while cutting wood beside a river, his axe slipped from his hands and fell into the deep water. The woodcutter sat sadly because he could not afford to buy a new axe.\r\n</div><div>Suddenly, a fairy appeared and offered to help him. The fairy dived into the river and brought out a golden axe. The woodcutter shook his head and said it was not his. Then the fairy brought out a silver axe, but again he refused it. Finally, the fairy brought out an old iron axe, and the woodcutter happily claimed it as his own.\r\n</div><div>The fairy was impressed by his honesty and gave him all three axes as a reward.\r\n</div><div><br></div><div>Why did the fairy reward the woodcutter?</div></p>','Because he was poor','Because he worked very hard','Because he told the truth','Because he helped the fairy<div><br></div>','Because he found the axes','','C'),(34281,'822',13,'PAT_9516','pg','<p>The Honest Woodcutter</p><p>\r\n<div>Once upon a time, a poor woodcutter lived near a forest. One day, while cutting wood beside a river, his axe slipped from his hands and fell into the deep water. The woodcutter sat sadly because he could not afford to buy a new axe.\r\n</div><div>Suddenly, a fairy appeared and offered to help him. The fairy dived into the river and brought out a golden axe. The woodcutter shook his head and said it was not his. Then the fairy brought out a silver axe, but again he refused it. Finally, the fairy brought out an old iron axe, and the woodcutter happily claimed it as his own.\r\n</div><div>The fairy was impressed by his honesty and gave him all three axes as a reward.\r\n</div><div><br></div><div>What would MOST LIKELY happen if the woodcutter claimed the golden axe?</div><div><br></div></p>','The fairy would still reward him','The fairy would disappear immediately','The woodcutter would become rich','The fairy might punish him for being dishonest','The river would dry up\r\n<div><br></div>','','D'),(34282,'822',14,'PAT_9516','pg','<p>The Honest Woodcutter</p><p>\r\n<div>Once upon a time, a poor woodcutter lived near a forest. One day, while cutting wood beside a river, his axe slipped from his hands and fell into the deep water. The woodcutter sat sadly because he could not afford to buy a new axe.\r\n</div><div>Suddenly, a fairy appeared and offered to help him. The fairy dived into the river and brought out a golden axe. The woodcutter shook his head and said it was not his. Then the fairy brought out a silver axe, but again he refused it. Finally, the fairy brought out an old iron axe, and the woodcutter happily claimed it as his own.\r\n</div><div>The fairy was impressed by his honesty and gave him all three axes as a reward.\r\n</div><div><br></div><div>Which statement BEST describes the moral value of the story?</div></p>','Hard work always makes people rich','Magical creatures can solve problems','Poverty causes dishonesty','Honesty brings positive outcomes','Expensive things create happiness\r\n<div><br></div>','','D'),(34283,'822',15,'PAT_9516','pg','<p>The Honest Woodcutter</p><p>\r\n<div>Once upon a time, a poor woodcutter lived near a forest. One day, while cutting wood beside a river, his axe slipped from his hands and fell into the deep water. The woodcutter sat sadly because he could not afford to buy a new axe.\r\n</div><div>Suddenly, a fairy appeared and offered to help him. The fairy dived into the river and brought out a golden axe. The woodcutter shook his head and said it was not his. Then the fairy brought out a silver axe, but again he refused it. Finally, the fairy brought out an old iron axe, and the woodcutter happily claimed it as his own.\r\n</div><div>The fairy was impressed by his honesty and gave him all three axes as a reward.\r\n</div><div><br></div><div>The main conflict in the story is …</div></p>','the woodcutter fighting the fairy','the fairy losing her axes','the woodcutter losing his axe in the river','the villagers mocking the woodcutter<div><br></div>','the river destroying the forest','','C'),(34284,'822',16,'PAT_9516','pg','<p>The Lion and the Mouse</p><p>\r\n<div>A lion was sleeping peacefully in the jungle when a little mouse accidentally ran across his body. Angry and disturbed, the lion caught the mouse and was about to eat him.\r\n</div><div>The frightened mouse begged for mercy and promised to help the lion someday. The lion laughed because he thought such a tiny creature could never help a powerful lion. Still, he let the mouse go.\r\n</div><div>A few days later, the lion was trapped in a hunter’s net. Hearing the lion’s roar, the mouse quickly came and bit the ropes until the lion escaped.\r\n</div><div>The lion finally realized that even the smallest friend could be valuable.\r\n</div><div><br></div><div>Why did the lion laugh at the mouse?\r\n</div><div><br></div></p>','The mouse looked funny','The mouse spoke rudely','The lion underestimated the mouse','The lion enjoyed helping animals','The mouse was trapped in a net\r\n<div><br></div>','','C'),(34285,'822',17,'PAT_9516','pg','<p>The Lion and the Mouse</p><p>\r\n<div>A lion was sleeping peacefully in the jungle when a little mouse accidentally ran across his body. Angry and disturbed, the lion caught the mouse and was about to eat him.\r\n</div><div>The frightened mouse begged for mercy and promised to help the lion someday. The lion laughed because he thought such a tiny creature could never help a powerful lion. Still, he let the mouse go.\r\n</div><div>A few days later, the lion was trapped in a hunter’s net. Hearing the lion’s roar, the mouse quickly came and bit the ropes until the lion escaped.\r\n</div><div>The lion finally realized that even the smallest friend could be valuable.\r\n</div><div><br></div><div>What lesson can readers learn from the story?</div></p>','Powerful people never need help','Small actions can have meaningful impacts','Wild animals should avoid humans','Friends should never make promises<div><br></div>','Strength is the key to survival','','B'),(34286,'822',18,'PAT_9516','pg','<p>The Lion and the Mouse</p><p>\r\n<div>A lion was sleeping peacefully in the jungle when a little mouse accidentally ran across his body. Angry and disturbed, the lion caught the mouse and was about to eat him.\r\n</div><div>The frightened mouse begged for mercy and promised to help the lion someday. The lion laughed because he thought such a tiny creature could never help a powerful lion. Still, he let the mouse go.\r\n</div><div>A few days later, the lion was trapped in a hunter’s net. Hearing the lion’s roar, the mouse quickly came and bit the ropes until the lion escaped.\r\n</div><div>The lion finally realized that even the smallest friend could be valuable.\r\n</div><div><br></div><div>Which event represents the climax of the story?</div></p>','The lion catches the mouse','The mouse runs through the jungle','The lion laughs loudly','The lion becomes trapped in the hunter’s net','The mouse asks for mercy\r\n<div><br></div>','','D'),(34287,'822',19,'PAT_9516','pg','<p>The Lion and the Mouse</p><p>\r\n<div>A lion was sleeping peacefully in the jungle when a little mouse accidentally ran across his body. Angry and disturbed, the lion caught the mouse and was about to eat him.\r\n</div><div>The frightened mouse begged for mercy and promised to help the lion someday. The lion laughed because he thought such a tiny creature could never help a powerful lion. Still, he let the mouse go.\r\n</div><div>A few days later, the lion was trapped in a hunter’s net. Hearing the lion’s roar, the mouse quickly came and bit the ropes until the lion escaped.\r\n</div><div>The lion finally realized that even the smallest friend could be valuable.\r\n</div><div><br></div><div>The word “valuable” in the last paragraph is closest in meaning to …</div></p>','useless','important','dangerous','expensive','weak\r\n<div><br></div>','','B'),(34288,'822',20,'PAT_9516','pg','<p>The Lion and the Mouse</p><p>\r\n<div>A lion was sleeping peacefully in the jungle when a little mouse accidentally ran across his body. Angry and disturbed, the lion caught the mouse and was about to eat him.\r\n</div><div>The frightened mouse begged for mercy and promised to help the lion someday. The lion laughed because he thought such a tiny creature could never help a powerful lion. Still, he let the mouse go.\r\n</div><div>A few days later, the lion was trapped in a hunter’s net. Hearing the lion’s roar, the mouse quickly came and bit the ropes until the lion escaped.\r\n</div><div>The lion finally realized that even the smallest friend could be valuable.\r\n</div><div><br></div><div>What might happen if the lion had eaten the mouse?</div></p>','<p>The lion might remain trapped in the net</p>','The lion would never get trapped','The hunters would free the lion','Another lion would help him<div><br></div>','The mouse would return as a ghost','','A'),(34289,'822',21,'PAT_9516','pg','<p>Beyond the Ground: Elevating Street Art</p><p>\r\n<div>\r\n</div><div>Street art has transcended conventional boundaries through graffiti-defying creations. By utilizing walls and vertical surfaces as canvases, artists challenge graffiti and engage viewers. This technique empowers artists to push creative limits and craft immersive experiences.\r\n</div><div>Graffiti art enriches the interaction between artists, artwork and surroundings. By incorporating urban architecture, artists initiate dialogues between art and public spaces. This heightens awareness of art\'s significance in daily life and invites viewers to participate in the creative process.\r\n</div><div>Notable examples include Banksy\'s and Shepard Fairey\'s works, leveraging graffiti art to convey powerful social and political messages. Thus, graffiti art becomes not only artistic expression but also a catalyst for social change.\r\n</div><div><br></div><div>The word “transcended” in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning (synonym) to…</div></p>','followed','went beyond','limited','destroyed<div><br></div>','reduced','','B'),(34290,'822',22,'PAT_9516','pg','<p>Beyond the Ground: Elevating Street Art</p><p>\r\n<div>\r\n</div><div>Street art has transcended conventional boundaries through graffiti-defying creations. By utilizing walls and vertical surfaces as canvases, artists challenge graffiti and engage viewers. This technique empowers artists to push creative limits and craft immersive experiences.\r\n</div><div>Graffiti art enriches the interaction between artists, artwork and surroundings. By incorporating urban architecture, artists initiate dialogues between art and public spaces. This heightens awareness of art\'s significance in daily life and invites viewers to participate in the creative process.\r\n</div><div>Notable examples include Banksy\'s and Shepard Fairey\'s works, leveraging graffiti art to convey powerful social and political messages. Thus, graffiti art becomes not only artistic expression but also a catalyst for social change.\r\n</div><div><br></div><div>The word “empowers” is opposite in meaning (antonym) to…</div></p>','supports','strengthens','weakens','encourages','inspires\r\n<div><br></div>','','C'),(34291,'822',23,'PAT_9516','pg','<p>Beyond the Ground: Elevating Street Art</p><p>\r\n<div>\r\n</div><div>Street art has transcended conventional boundaries through graffiti-defying creations. By utilizing walls and vertical surfaces as canvases, artists challenge graffiti and engage viewers. This technique empowers artists to push creative limits and craft immersive experiences.\r\n</div><div>Graffiti art enriches the interaction between artists, artwork and surroundings. By incorporating urban architecture, artists initiate dialogues between art and public spaces. This heightens awareness of art\'s significance in daily life and invites viewers to participate in the creative process.\r\n</div><div>Notable examples include Banksy\'s and Shepard Fairey\'s works, leveraging graffiti art to convey powerful social and political messages. Thus, graffiti art becomes not only artistic expression but also a catalyst for social change.\r\n</div><div><br></div><div>The word “immersive” is closest in meaning (synonym) to…</div></p>','engaging','boring','simple','ordinary\r\n<div><br></div>','distant','','A'),(34292,'822',24,'PAT_9516','pg','<p>Beyond the Ground: Elevating Street Art</p><p>\r\n<div>\r\n</div><div>Street art has transcended conventional boundaries through graffiti-defying creations. By utilizing walls and vertical surfaces as canvases, artists challenge graffiti and engage viewers. This technique empowers artists to push creative limits and craft immersive experiences.\r\n</div><div>Graffiti art enriches the interaction between artists, artwork and surroundings. By incorporating urban architecture, artists initiate dialogues between art and public spaces. This heightens awareness of art\'s significance in daily life and invites viewers to participate in the creative process.\r\n</div><div>Notable examples include Banksy\'s and Shepard Fairey\'s works, leveraging graffiti art to convey powerful social and political messages. Thus, graffiti art becomes not only artistic expression but also a catalyst for social change.\r\n</div><div><br></div><div>The word “enriches” is opposite in meaning (antonym) to…</div></p>','improves','enhances','develops','damages<div><br></div>','increases','','D'),(34293,'822',25,'PAT_9516','pg','<p>Beyond the Ground: Elevating Street Art</p><p>\r\n<div>\r\n</div><div>Street art has transcended conventional boundaries through graffiti-defying creations. By utilizing walls and vertical surfaces as canvases, artists challenge graffiti and engage viewers. This technique empowers artists to push creative limits and craft immersive experiences.\r\n</div><div>Graffiti art enriches the interaction between artists, artwork and surroundings. By incorporating urban architecture, artists initiate dialogues between art and public spaces. This heightens awareness of art\'s significance in daily life and invites viewers to participate in the creative process.\r\n</div><div>Notable examples include Banksy\'s and Shepard Fairey\'s works, leveraging graffiti art to convey powerful social and political messages. Thus, graffiti art becomes not only artistic expression but also a catalyst for social change.\r\n</div><div><br></div><div>The word “significance” is closest in meaning (synonym) to…</div><div>\r\n</div></p>','importance','difficulty','weakness','confusion','failure\r\n<div><br></div>','','A'),(34294,'822',26,'PAT_9516','pg','<p>History of Graffiti: Is it Art?</p><p>\r\n<div>The term graffiti comes from the Greek, word of \'graphein\' which means \'to write\'. People have always drawn and painted on walls. Prehistoric man painted on the walls of caves, and those paintings are now considered valuable works of art. Graffiti was first found on ancient Roman architecture.\r\n</div><div>If people draw on walls these days, it isn\'t considered art; it is called \'graffiti\' and often cleaned off as soon as possible. Graffiti art takes a number of forms. It can be an individual mark, usually just the initials of the artist, or something much larger, like a mural. Most graffiti artists prefer to be called \'writers\'. A writer\'s signature with spray paint or maker is called a \'tag\', and each piece of graffiti art is \'tagged\'. Communities of writers who are friends are known as \'crews\', and inexperienced writers are called \'toys\'.\r\n</div><div>For some artists graffiti is a reaction to the circumstances of their lives, while for others it is a more thought-out artistic expression. The motivation behind a piece of graffiti can be happiness or sadness, frustration or relief. For this reason, it is an art form that is very close to the hearts of the young.\r\n</div><div>Graffiti as a youth culture began in America but has become popular in many other countries. Its influences, come from pop culture especially music and cartoons. Graffiti is now so popular; it can be seen in many museums and art galleries.\r\n</div><div><br></div><div>What is the social function (purpose) of an analytical exposition text?</div></p>','To entertain the reader with a story','To describe a particular person or place','To persuade the reader that something is the case','To explain how something works<div><br></div>','To retell past experiences','','C'),(34295,'822',27,'PAT_9516','pg','<p>History of Graffiti: Is it Art?</p><p>\r\n<div>The term graffiti comes from the Greek, word of \'graphein\' which means \'to write\'. People have always drawn and painted on walls. Prehistoric man painted on the walls of caves, and those paintings are now considered valuable works of art. Graffiti was first found on ancient Roman architecture.\r\n</div><div>If people draw on walls these days, it isn\'t considered art; it is called \'graffiti\' and often cleaned off as soon as possible. Graffiti art takes a number of forms. It can be an individual mark, usually just the initials of the artist, or something much larger, like a mural. Most graffiti artists prefer to be called \'writers\'. A writer\'s signature with spray paint or maker is called a \'tag\', and each piece of graffiti art is \'tagged\'. Communities of writers who are friends are known as \'crews\', and inexperienced writers are called \'toys\'.\r\n</div><div>For some artists graffiti is a reaction to the circumstances of their lives, while for others it is a more thought-out artistic expression. The motivation behind a piece of graffiti can be happiness or sadness, frustration or relief. For this reason, it is an art form that is very close to the hearts of the young.\r\n</div><div>Graffiti as a youth culture began in America but has become popular in many other countries. Its influences, come from pop culture especially music and cartoons. Graffiti is now so popular; it can be seen in many museums and art galleries.\r\n</div><div><br></div><div>Which part of analytical exposition contains the writer’s arguments?</div></p>','Orientation','Complication','Arguments','Resolution<div><br></div>','Recount','','C'),(34296,'822',28,'PAT_9516','pg','<p>History of Graffiti: Is it Art?</p><p>\r\n<div>The term graffiti comes from the Greek, word of \'graphein\' which means \'to write\'. People have always drawn and painted on walls. Prehistoric man painted on the walls of caves, and those paintings are now considered valuable works of art. Graffiti was first found on ancient Roman architecture.\r\n</div><div>If people draw on walls these days, it isn\'t considered art; it is called \'graffiti\' and often cleaned off as soon as possible. Graffiti art takes a number of forms. It can be an individual mark, usually just the initials of the artist, or something much larger, like a mural. Most graffiti artists prefer to be called \'writers\'. A writer\'s signature with spray paint or maker is called a \'tag\', and each piece of graffiti art is \'tagged\'. Communities of writers who are friends are known as \'crews\', and inexperienced writers are called \'toys\'.\r\n</div><div>For some artists graffiti is a reaction to the circumstances of their lives, while for others it is a more thought-out artistic expression. The motivation behind a piece of graffiti can be happiness or sadness, frustration or relief. For this reason, it is an art form that is very close to the hearts of the young.\r\n</div><div>Graffiti as a youth culture began in America but has become popular in many other countries. Its influences, come from pop culture especially music and cartoons. Graffiti is now so popular; it can be seen in many museums and art galleries.</div><div><br></div><div>\r\n</div><div>What is an analytical exposition text?</div></p>','A text that tells past events in chronological order<div><br></div>','A text that describes a person, place, or thing in detail','A text that explains how to do something step by step','A text that presents arguments to persuade the reader that something is true','A text that entertains readers with imaginative stories','','D'),(34297,'822',29,'PAT_9516','pg','<p>History of Graffiti: Is it Art?</p><p>\r\n<div>The term graffiti comes from the Greek, word of \'graphein\' which means \'to write\'. People have always drawn and painted on walls. Prehistoric man painted on the walls of caves, and those paintings are now considered valuable works of art. Graffiti was first found on ancient Roman architecture.\r\n</div><div>If people draw on walls these days, it isn\'t considered art; it is called \'graffiti\' and often cleaned off as soon as possible. Graffiti art takes a number of forms. It can be an individual mark, usually just the initials of the artist, or something much larger, like a mural. Most graffiti artists prefer to be called \'writers\'. A writer\'s signature with spray paint or maker is called a \'tag\', and each piece of graffiti art is \'tagged\'. Communities of writers who are friends are known as \'crews\', and inexperienced writers are called \'toys\'.\r\n</div><div>For some artists graffiti is a reaction to the circumstances of their lives, while for others it is a more thought-out artistic expression. The motivation behind a piece of graffiti can be happiness or sadness, frustration or relief. For this reason, it is an art form that is very close to the hearts of the young.\r\n</div><div>Graffiti as a youth culture began in America but has become popular in many other countries. Its influences, come from pop culture especially music and cartoons. Graffiti is now so popular; it can be seen in many museums and art galleries.\r\n</div><div><br></div><div>What does the text suggest about valuable works of art?</div></p>','Valuable works of art only come from modern cities.','Valuable works of art include prehistoric paintings on cave walls.','Valuable works of art are always created by professional artists.<div><br></div>','Valuable works of art are usually graffiti found on buildings today.','Valuable works of art only exist in museums and galleries.','','B'),(34298,'822',30,'PAT_9516','pg','<p>History of Graffiti: Is it Art?</p><p>\r\n<div>The term graffiti comes from the Greek, word of \'graphein\' which means \'to write\'. People have always drawn and painted on walls. Prehistoric man painted on the walls of caves, and those paintings are now considered valuable works of art. Graffiti was first found on ancient Roman architecture.\r\n</div><div>If people draw on walls these days, it isn\'t considered art; it is called \'graffiti\' and often cleaned off as soon as possible. Graffiti art takes a number of forms. It can be an individual mark, usually just the initials of the artist, or something much larger, like a mural. Most graffiti artists prefer to be called \'writers\'. A writer\'s signature with spray paint or maker is called a \'tag\', and each piece of graffiti art is \'tagged\'. Communities of writers who are friends are known as \'crews\', and inexperienced writers are called \'toys\'.\r\n</div><div>For some artists graffiti is a reaction to the circumstances of their lives, while for others it is a more thought-out artistic expression. The motivation behind a piece of graffiti can be happiness or sadness, frustration or relief. For this reason, it is an art form that is very close to the hearts of the young.\r\n</div><div>Graffiti as a youth culture began in America but has become popular in many other countries. Its influences, come from pop culture especially music and cartoons. Graffiti is now so popular; it can be seen in many museums and art galleries.\r\n</div><div><br></div><div>What is the main idea of the second paragraph?</div></p>','The history of graffiti in ancient times','The influence of music on graffiti culture','The reasons why young people like graffiti','The popularity of graffiti in museums<div><br></div>','Different forms and terms used in graffiti art','','E'),(34299,'822',31,'PAT_9516','pg','He does not like graffiti.\r\n<div>The correct interrogative form is…</div><div><br></div>','Does she like graffiti?','Do she like graffiti?','Is she like graffiti?','Does she likes graffiti?','Did she like graffiti?','','A'),(34300,'822',32,'PAT_9516','pg','Is she happy with the graffiti result?\r\n<div>The correct positive sentence is…</div>','She am happy with the graffiti result.','She is happy with the graffiti result.','She are happy with the graffiti result.','She happy with the graffiti result.<div><br></div>','She be happy with the graffiti result.','','B'),(34301,'822',33,'PAT_9516','pg','She is happy with the painting.\r\n<div>The correct negative and interrogative forms are…</div>','She is not happy with the painting. / Is she happy with the painting?','She not is happy with the painting. / Does she happy with the painting?','She are not happy with the painting. / Are she happy with the painting?','<p>&nbsp;She is not happy with the painting. / Do she happy with the painting?</p>','She am not happy with the painting. / Am she happy with the painting?\r\n<div><br></div>','','A'),(34302,'822',34,'PAT_9516','pg','<p>Read the statements below carefully!</p><p><div>1. Narrative text tells readers about past events.</div><div>2. Narrative text aims to entertain and teach moral values.</div><div>3. Narrative text only explains scientific facts.</div><div>4. Narrative text usually contains conflicts and resolutions.</div><div><br></div><div>Which statements BEST describe a narrative text?</div></p>','1 and 2','2 and 3','2 and 4','1, 2, and 4','1, 3, and 4','','D'),(34303,'822',35,'PAT_9516','pg','What is the main purpose of a narrative text?','To explain how to make something','To entertain readers through a story and sometimes give moral lessons','To describe a particular person or place in detail','To inform readers about scientific facts','To persuade readers to agree with an opinion','','B'),(34304,'822',36,'PAT_9516','pg','The Greedy Farmer<div><br></div><div>A farmer found a goose that laid a golden egg every morning. At first, he was very happy because he became rich slowly. However, after several months, he became impatient. He thought that there must be many golden eggs inside the goose’s body.</div><div>One day, he killed the goose and cut it open. Unfortunately, he found nothing inside. Realizing his mistake, the farmer cried because he had lost the goose forever.</div><div><br></div><div>Why did the farmer kill the goose?</div>','He wanted to sell the goose immediately','He was afraid the goose would run away','He hoped to get all the golden eggs at once','He wanted to give the goose to someone else','He thought the goose was dangerous','','C'),(34305,'822',37,'PAT_9516','pg','The Greedy Farmer<div><br></div><div>A farmer found a goose that laid a golden egg every morning. At first, he was very happy because he became rich slowly. However, after several months, he became impatient. He thought that there must be many golden eggs inside the goose’s body.</div><div>One day, he killed the goose and cut it open. Unfortunately, he found nothing inside. Realizing his mistake, the farmer cried because he had lost the goose forever.</div><div><br></div><div>Which statement BEST explains the moral value of the story?</div>','Wealth always brings happiness','Greed can lead to loss','Animals should be protected','Farmers must work harder','Gold is more important than honesty','','B'),(34306,'822',38,'PAT_9516','pg','The Boy Who Cried Wolf<div><br></div><div>A shepherd boy was bored while watching his sheep near a village. To amuse himself, he shouted, “Wolf! Wolf!” The villagers ran to help him, but they found no wolf at all. The boy laughed at them.</div><div>A few days later, he repeated the same trick. Again, the villagers came and realized that the boy had lied. They became angry and warned him not to do it again.</div><div>One afternoon, a real wolf appeared and attacked the sheep. The boy screamed for help as loudly as he could, but this time no one believed him. In the end, many sheep were lost.</div><div><br></div><div>Why did the villagers ignore the boy’s last cry for help?</div>','They were too busy working','They did not hear the boy’s voice','They believed the wolf was harmless','They no longer trusted the boy','They wanted to punish the sheep','','D'),(34307,'822',39,'PAT_9516','pg','The Village of Endless Light<div><br></div><div>Long ago, there was a small village surrounded by mountains. The villagers depended on oil lamps to light their houses at night. Unfortunately, oil was expensive and difficult to find.</div><div>A clever girl named Amira wanted to help her village. One day, she noticed the strong flow of water in the river and got an idea to create a water wheel that could produce electricity.</div><div>Many villagers doubted her idea, but Amira continued working hard. After several weeks, the water wheel finally produced electricity for some houses. The villagers were amazed and began working together to build more water wheels.</div><div>Since then, the village had enough energy, and children could study comfortably at night.</div><div><br></div><div>Why did Amira decide to create a water wheel?</div>','She wanted to become famous in the village','She was interested in building toys','She wanted to solve the village’s energy problem','She wanted to sell electricity to other villages','She was forced by the villagers','','C'),(34308,'822',40,'PAT_9516','pg','The Village of Endless Light<div><br></div><div>Long ago, there was a small village surrounded by mountains. The villagers depended on oil lamps to light their houses at night. Unfortunately, oil was expensive and difficult to find.</div><div>A clever girl named Amira wanted to help her village. One day, she noticed the strong flow of water in the river and got an idea to create a water wheel that could produce electricity.</div><div>Many villagers doubted her idea, but Amira continued working hard. After several weeks, the water wheel finally produced electricity for some houses. The villagers were amazed and began working together to build more water wheels.</div><div>Since then, the village had enough energy, and children could study comfortably at night.</div><div><br></div><div>Why did the villagers doubt Amira at first?</div>','They did not believe a young girl could solve the problem','They hated new technology','They already had enough electricity','They wanted to leave the village','They were afraid of the river','','A'),(34309,'822',41,'PAT_9516','uraian','Graffiti\'s Gray Area: Vandalism or Artistic Expression\r\n<div><br></div><div>Graffiti is often viewed as vandalism due to property damage. However, can all graffiti be classified as criminal acts? Or does it hold artistic and social value? These sparks debate about graffiti\'s status as art.\r\n</div><div>Graffiti can be authentic, spontaneous artistic expression. Artists utilize their work to convey vital social and political messages. Yet, without property owner consent, graffiti is considered vandalism.\r\n</div><div>Some graffiti has gained recognition as legitimate art, protected by law. Jean-Michel Basquiat\'s and Keith Haring\'s works demonstrate significant contributions. Therefore, graffiti\'s status warrants reevaluation.</div><div><br></div><div>\r\n</div><div>What is your opinion about the arguments presented in the text regarding graffiti as both vandalism and artistic expression? Do you think the arguments are convincing? Explain your reasons with supporting ideas.</div>','','','','','','','In my opinion, the arguments in the text are convincing because graffiti can have both negative and positive aspects. It is true that graffiti without permission can damage property and be considered vandalism. However, graffiti can also be a powerful form of artistic expression that communicates social and political messages. Many famous artists started with graffiti, which shows its artistic value. Therefore, I believe graffiti should not always be judged as a crime.'),(34310,'822',42,'PAT_9516','uraian','<p>Graffiti\'s Gray Area: Vandalism or Artistic Expression</p><p>\r\n<div>\r\n</div><div>Graffiti is often viewed as vandalism due to property damage. However, can all graffiti be classified as criminal acts? Or does it hold artistic and social value? These sparks debate about graffiti\'s status as art.\r\n</div><div>Graffiti can be authentic, spontaneous artistic expression. Artists utilize their work to convey vital social and political messages. Yet, without property owner consent, graffiti is considered vandalism.\r\n</div><div>Some graffiti has gained recognition as legitimate art, protected by law. Jean-Michel Basquiat\'s and Keith Haring\'s works demonstrate significant contributions. Therefore, graffiti\'s status warrants reevaluation.\r\n</div><div><br></div><div>The text mentions Jean-Michel Basquiat and Keith Haring. Why are they mentioned?\r\n</div><div><br></div></p>','','','','','','','They are mentioned because they are famous artists. Their success shows that graffiti can be real art.'),(34311,'822',43,'PAT_9516','uraian','The Power of the Sun<div><br></div><div>One day, a small village often had blackouts at night. The villagers could not study or work properly because there was not enough electricity.</div><div>A smart student named Bima thought about using sunlight as energy. He collected some old solar panels from a nearby city and tried to install them on the roof of his school. At first, many people doubted him because they thought the project was too difficult.</div><div>However, Bima kept learning and working hard. After several weeks, the solar panels finally worked. The school could produce electricity from sunlight, and students were able to study comfortably again.</div><div>The villagers were proud of Bima and started using solar energy in their homes. They realized that the sun could become a useful and environmentally friendly source of energy.</div><div><br></div><div>Why did many villagers doubt Bima’s idea at first?</div>','','','','','','','Because they thought producing electricity from sunlight was too difficult and impossible for a student.'),(34312,'822',44,'PAT_9516','uraian','The Power of the Sun<div><br></div><div>One day, a small village often had blackouts at night. The villagers could not study or work properly because there was not enough electricity.</div><div>A smart student named Bima thought about using sunlight as energy. He collected some old solar panels from a nearby city and tried to install them on the roof of his school. At first, many people doubted him because they thought the project was too difficult.</div><div>However, Bima kept learning and working hard. After several weeks, the solar panels finally worked. The school could produce electricity from sunlight, and students were able to study comfortably again.</div><div>The villagers were proud of Bima and started using solar energy in their homes. They realized that the sun could become a useful and environmentally friendly source of energy.</div><div><br></div><div>What can readers learn from Bima’s character?</div>','','','','','','','Readers can learn to be creative, hardworking, and never give up when solving problems.'),(34313,'822',45,'PAT_9516','uraian','The Power of the Sun<div><br></div><div>One day, a small village often had blackouts at night. The villagers could not study or work properly because there was not enough electricity.</div><div>A smart student named Bima thought about using sunlight as energy. He collected some old solar panels from a nearby city and tried to install them on the roof of his school. At first, many people doubted him because they thought the project was too difficult.</div><div>However, Bima kept learning and working hard. After several weeks, the solar panels finally worked. The school could produce electricity from sunlight, and students were able to study comfortably again.</div><div>The villagers were proud of Bima and started using solar energy in their homes. They realized that the sun could become a useful and environmentally friendly source of energy.</div><div><br></div><div>In your opinion, how can students help save energy at school?</div>','','','','','','','Students can turn off lights and electronic devices when not used and support the use of renewable energy like solar panels.'),(34314,'823',1,'PAT_9517','pg','<p>Graffiti: Art or Problem?</p><p>\r\n<div>Graffiti is common in many cities today. Some people think graffiti is art, but others think it is a problem. I believe graffiti on public places without permission should be banned.\r\n</div><div>First, graffiti makes public places look dirty and damaged. Cleaning it also costs money. Second, too much graffiti can make people feel uncomfortable because the area looks unsafe. However, graffiti can be positive if it is made legally in special areas for artists.\r\n</div><div>In conclusion, graffiti without permission should not be allowed, but legal graffiti can still be appreciated as art.\r\n</div><div><br></div><div>What is the writer’s main opinion?</div></p>','Graffiti is always beautiful art.','Graffiti should be free everywhere.','Graffiti without permission should be banned.','Graffiti artists should be arrested.<div><br></div>','Graffiti has no negative impact.','','C'),(34315,'823',2,'PAT_9517','pg','<div>Graffiti: Art or Problem?</div><div><br></div><div>\r\n</div><div>Graffiti is common in many cities today. Some people think graffiti is art, but others think it is a problem. I believe graffiti on public places without permission should be banned.\r\n</div><div>First, graffiti makes public places look dirty and damaged. Cleaning it also costs money. Second, too much graffiti can make people feel uncomfortable because the area looks unsafe. However, graffiti can be positive if it is made legally in special 